      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y′=\\frac{3 - 2 x}{y}.$ Work on intervals where $y\\ne 0$ and the radicand is positive.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y′ & =\\frac{3 - 2 x}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =3 - 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\mathrm{d}y & =\\int 3 - 2 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ \\frac{y^{2}}{2} & =- x^{2} + 3 x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution in implicit form is\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2}=- x^{2} + 3 x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ Solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^2 & =- 2 x^{2} + 6 x+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{- 2 x^{2} + 6 x+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm\\sqrt{- 2 x^{2} + 6 x+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C$ is an arbitrary real constant.",
