      "title": "Finding a General Solution in Explicit Form"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3e84e304-2fac-582f-835a-7caa074527bd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t}=-0.03(T-35)$ for $T\\ne 35.$\n\n$T=35+K\\cdot$ {{field-1}}, where $K$ is an arbitrary nonzero constant.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03(T-35) \\\\ \\frac{1}{T-35}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{T-35}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int -0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-35} & =-0.03\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-35| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $T=T(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $T.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-35| & =-0.03t+C \\\\ |T-35| & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ |T-35| & =K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary positive constant.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be both positive and negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =35+K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8aa545f0-213f-5654-8a30-bda720ed4cb3",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
